(project tuna-purity
 (* agent note (|2026-10-01, M10+ experiment: the RUNTIME PURITY THESIS
    (tuna.borg docstring; .ralph/plan.md Open Questions) is realized as
    Tuna_interp.Flat — a pure explicit-stack (CEK-style) core whose
    state is a value, no monad parameter and no Lwt/scheduler types
    anywhere under common/, interpreter/, compiler/.  The corpus
    referee (tests/flat_tests.ml) requires bit-for-bit step-count
    identity with the recursive monadic engine (Prim_eval.Make) across
    the whole differential corpus under BOTH laws (Canonical v0 and
    Sharing v1), plus prim/dirty/loop/determinism/step-by-step/async-
    suspension cases — 8 green.  Cost probe (tools/flatbench): the two
    workloads the thesis names (small-prim, prim-heavy) measure within
    noise of the recursive engine (flat/rec ratio 0.47–1.24 across
    1e2–2e4 boundary crossings) with identical step counts — nothing
    aesthetic, measured.  The suspension property the explicit-stack
    shape was chosen for is a tested fact: a prim call parks the
    machine in a [pending] VALUE the driver answers out of band, with
    no scheduler in the core.  Either outcome was pre-registered as a
    valid finding; the observed one is "the pure-step core is viable",
    recorded here and in FINDINGS.md.  MIGRATION COMPLETE 2026-10-01:
    the server run boundary (Run.execute) and the replay/counterfactual
    engine (Replay.execute_fed) now drive the pure core through
    Tuna_interp.Flat_drive.Make(Lwt) — the monad lives at the rim
    (the host adapter), not in the core.  The public pure API
    (Tuna_interp.Eval) also delegates to the flat driver.  The
    recursive Prim_eval.Make is KEPT as the corpus referee the tests
    cross-check against (tests/flat_tests.ml).  Full battery with the
    migrated boundary: pure suites + PG suites + differential 24/24 +
    smoke-api 14/14 + smoke-ui 17/17 + verify-1..7 7/7 (replay identity
    included).|) *)
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: purity — the pure-step core (RUNTIME PURITY THESIS). An explicit-stack abstract machine whose state is a value: one elementary reduction per step, no scheduler, no monad in the core. The corpus referees that its step counts are identical to the recursive engine under both accounting laws.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section pure-step-core
  (doc "state is a value; step : machine -> machine option; no monad, no Lwt")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The end-state shape the thesis names: [machine] is a record of
        control (an optional [apply f x]), a continuation stack ([k]),
        the current value, budget, and optional v1 memo state.  [step]
        performs exactly ONE elementary reduction — a wrapper
        application, a triage firing, or the SUSPENSION of the machine
        on a prim call — and returns the successor state, or [None] when
        halted/already suspended.  Nothing in the module names a
        scheduler or a monad: it is plain OCaml.

        Suspension is the payoff of the shape: a prim call parks the
        machine in [pending_ : (site * name * args) option], a VALUE the
        driver inspects, holds, or answers out of band via [answer].
        [run ~host] is sugar over [step]/[answer] for a synchronous host;
        [advance] stops at the next suspension.  A host that wants to do
        I/O simply stops calling [step] and resumes with [answer] — no
        callback, no monad, no concealed promise runtime.

        The recursive monadic engine (Prim_eval.Make) is KEPT as the
        corpus referee the thesis designates, and the migration has
        landed: the server run boundary (Run.execute) and the replay
        engine (Replay.execute_fed) drive the pure core through
        Tuna_interp.Flat_drive.Make(Lwt) — a thin host adapter that
        sequences pure [step]/[answer] calls in Lwt.  The public pure
        API (Tuna_interp.Eval) delegates to the flat driver too, via the
        identity-monad instantiation.  So the monad now lives ONLY at
        the rim: the core names no scheduler; the host adapter owns the
        effects.  tests/flat_tests.ml cross-checks the pure core against
        the retained recursive engine on the whole differential corpus
        under both laws, and the acceptance scripts (verify-3 replay
        identity included) run green over the migrated boundary.
       |))))
 (section host-adapter
  (doc "Flat_drive: the monad lives at the rim, never in the core")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Flat_drive.Make(M) is the bridge the thesis called the
        migration stage's successor.  It has API parity with
        Prim_eval.Make: [eval ~host ~mode ~fuel ~size_cap ~program args]
        returns a Prim_eval.result in M, with a monadic host of the
        [`Ok | `Error] M.t shape.  Inside, [drive] is a tail-recursive
        bind chain: if the pure machine is parked on a prim, it awaits
        the host's M answer and injects it with Flat.answer; otherwise
        it takes one pure Flat.step.  No evaluation state lives in the
        adapter — only the sequencing.

        The wall-clock deadline (AGENTS.md rule 5) is enforced HERE,
        between elementary steps, never inside the core: pure
        evaluation stays untimed and step counts stay an invariant.
        A boundary abort finalizes the machine as Deadline_exceeded via
        Flat.abort — an operator-policy state transition, exactly like
        the recursive engine's exception, but expressed as a value.

        Swapping the engine at Run/Replay was a one-line module change
        each because of the API parity, which is the point: the thesis
        could be realized without rewriting the boundary.
       |))))
 (section corpus-referee
  (doc "step counts identical to the recursive engine, both laws")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        tests/flat_tests.ml is the referee: for every
        scripts/diff-corpus entry it runs BOTH engines and requires the
        (status, result-ternary, steps) triple to agree exactly, under
        Canonical (v0) and Sharing (v1).  The flat machine is an
        INDEPENDENT implementation — it re-implements the counting law
        rather than calling the recursive engine — which is what makes
        agreement meaningful.

        The order is the verbatim one (AGENTS.md rule 4): wrappers are
        application not steps; every triage firing costs one step and
        one fuel; fork(stem a1,a2) evaluates a1 c THEN a2 c THEN the
        outer apply (the [KStemFirst]/[KStemSecond] frames encode
        exactly this); fork(fork,a3) dispatches free; prim calls are
        fuel-free and step-free.

        Plus: prim boundary (denial answer tree), dirty firing under v1
        (a prim answered inside a firing never memoizes), loop law
        (self-application answers Loop at the same step), determinism
        (pure ⇒ two runs agree), step-by-step (stepping to completion
        one reduction at a time equals [run]), async suspension (a
        scheduler-free driver answers a parked prim).

        Failure definition carried over from the thesis: if the flat
        machine cannot reproduce corpus step counts identical to the
        verbatim-port reference, that is a spec violation (corpus red),
        not a design variant.  It does, so it is not.
       |))))
 (section measured-cost
  (doc "the criterion is measured cost on equal step counts, not aesthetics")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        tools/flatbench (dune exec tools/flatbench/flatbench.exe)
        compares the two engines on the workloads the thesis names:

          - small-prim (mostly pure calculus, few boundary crossings),
            depths 100/500/2000;
          - prim-heavy (many boundary crossings), n = 1000/5000/20000.

        Observed 2026-10-01 (native, dev box): flat/rec wall-time ratios
        0.47–1.24 with IDENTICAL step counts throughout — i.e. within
        measurement noise, with the flat machine slightly ahead at the
        largest prim-heavy size (0.89) and in v1 sharing mode (0.47).
        The thesis pre-registered two possible outcomes (rim-handler
        O(depth) tax vs flat constant-per-step); the observed result is
        the flat machine is viable at no measured step-cost premium.
        The number is what matters, not the direction.
       |))))
)
